We employ the Franck-Condon approach accompanied by a variational method to study
polarons within a two-layered semiconductor system, which exhibits weakly interacting quasi-1D excitons.
Utilizing a Hamiltonian with truncated Hilbert space and bilayer separation potential, we calculate
effective mass enhancements for both weak-coupling regimes and when the coupling matches the intralayer
exciton Rydberg parameter. Additionally, we identify the lowest lying polariton state and estimate its energy
within these weak-coupling regimes. Our calculations for single-layer polarons are in line with existing literature. 
For bilayer polarons, we observe that effective mass enhancement increases with interlayer coupling
strength and find that in the strong coupling regime, energetic positioning of bilayer polarons is intermediate
between single-layer polarons and interlayer phonon parameters. Most intriguingly, we document a sudden rise
in effective mass enhancement when the coupling strength approaches (but does not exceed) the intralayer Rydberg
parameter, which may be reciprocated in other quasi-1D systems. Our work using the Franck-Condon approach
opens up avenues for further research on evaluating mass enhancements in novel 2D semiconductor systems. 

