In various computer-supported collaborative learning environments, researchers and knowledge workers share and analyze information to address complex challenges, such as developing innovative medical treatments. Advances in artificial intelligence are enhancing collaborative research capabilities by utilizing knowledge artifacts, like academic papers and database entries, to facilitate both foundational and applied intelligence. This paradigm leverages knowledge workers' input to improve AI models, which can then be employed to support fresh knowledge production and optimize existing intelligence. Although this cycle of knowledge enhancement is currently unsupervised and fragmented, our work seeks to integrate collaborative learning with knowledge enhancement via artificial intelligence through a unified concept, which we term 'cohesive collaborative intelligence.' We are demonstrating the efficacy of this idea by scoring the relevance of pairs of medical interventions using clinical co-occurrences and textual similarities found in academic publications, which serves as a simple demonstration of the vast possibilities of cohesive collaborative intelligence. Our research shows promising results and offers a clear pathway for future development, allowing others to build upon our findings and explore novel applications of this innovative framework. Ultimately, our goal is to empower AI systems with a profound understanding of collaborative learning dynamics, leading to superior performance in modeling human interaction, as well as to harness the full potential of artificial intelligence and collaborative information sharing. We are open to feedback and collaboration to further develop this framework in meaningful ways, such as applying it to identify leading researchers or predicting clinical trials, as exemplified by our novel score, which indicates a highly relevant medication pair has a 4 out of 5 chance of being used together, according to clinical data, which is n