Our approach to navigating complex environments relies on a deep learning-based sonar system, low-level motion control, and a real-time world map. This multi-layered system is comprised of a sensor module that merges sonar readings, a navigation module that updates the map and localizes the robot, and a high-level decision-making module. Although the sensor and navigation modules function independently, they share the goal of assisting the robot in achieving its objectives. By integrating with each other, these modules can enable the robot to interact with its surroundings. Our work involves incorporating real-time sonar data into the map module, allowing the robot to discover and adapt to new environments. This process could provide critical information to the high-level decision-making module, potentially enhancing decision-making throughout the mission. Our method for incorporating sonar data into the map is presented, along with initial results from a simulated environment. Due to hardware constraints, our system was tested in a controlled environment using fake sonar data. Future developments will involve integrating our system with a decision-making module and testing it in real-world settings. Our code is openly accessible on GitHub. 

Keywords: Navigation, Sonar, Machine Learning, Robot Control, Deep Learning. 

